    "q-279970": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279970",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258624/assets/32a9eefc6c23370b507418bb0a7a5e31d05287fa088281e44ef352bc3f25afd5.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Translate the graph to the right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-3}.$\n\n- Translate the result up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+2.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14258624/assets/8ca6a1b7f5f5c8e7f5eef4922845b84f30c599e4be41a02bd517200568c75a88.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
